emoved at surgery, characterized as adverse pathological findings discovered on testing of excised tissue after a local excision [Data: Entities (2783)].\\n\\nThese findings are explicitly linked to higher likelihood of residual disease or recurrence, which is why they are treated as a key determinant of whether escalation beyond local excision is warranted [Data: Entities (2783)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Pathology-based tissue testing is explicitly framed as a mechanism for detecting high-risk features\",\"explanation\":\"The dataset distinguishes a specific framing—“TISSUE TESTING (PATHOLOGY)”—as testing of removed polyp/tumor tissue to identify cancer and high-risk features, with results guiding whether additional resection or other treatment is needed [Data: Entities (2759)].\\n\\nA direct relationship asserts that pathology testing of removed tissues can identify high-risk features that affect subsequent management, reinforcing that the pathway is driven by post-excision diagnostic information rather than only pre-operative assessment [Data: Relationships (5121)].\"},{\"summary\":\"High-risk features are an explicit reason additional treatment may be recommended after local excision\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes an explicit linkage stating that high-risk pathology findings are listed as a reason more treatment may be needed after local excision [Data: Relationships (5140)].\\n\\nThis is consistent with the definition of additional treatment as further therapy beyond local excision recommended when pathology reveals high-risk features (or when the tumor is more advanced than expected), showing that the presence of these features is not merely informational but a stated decision criterion [Data: Entities (2784); Relationships (5140)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Additional treatment is defined as escalation beyond local excision and can include multiple therapeutic modalities\",\"explanation\":\"Additional treatment is defined as therapy beyond local excision that may be recommended when pathology reveals high-risk features or when the tumor is more advanced than initially expected (for example, T2 invasion) [Data: Entities (2784)].\\n\\nThe potential modalities listed include transabdominal surgery, chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or combinations, indicating that escalation can range from further surgery to systemic and/or radiation-based therapies depending on context [Data: Entities (2784)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The network contains a reinforcing bidirectional framing between high-risk features and additional treatment (reason-for-treatment linkage)\",\"explanation\":\"Beyond the direct relationship from high-risk features to additional treatment, the dataset also includes a reciprocal relationship that states high-risk features found on tissue testing are explicitly listed as a reason additional treatment may be needed after local excision [Data: Relationships (5197)].\\n\\nTaken together, these links emphasize the central role of pathology-driven risk stratification: high-risk features are both the output of tissue testing and a stated justification for therapy escalation, making this community a tightly coupled decision loop centered on post-excision findings [Data: Relationships (5160, 5140, 5197)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Overall structure is a linear escalation pathway: testing → risk identification → treatment escalation\",\"explanation\":\"At a structural level, the community can be summarized as a stepwise pathway: tissue testing (including pathology testing) identifies high-risk features, and those high-risk features are cited as a reason to recommend additional treatment [Data: Relationships (5160, 5121, 5140)].\\n\\nThis structure highlights a key operational dependency: downstream treatment intensity is contingent on what is discovered in excised tissue, aligning clinical management decisions with objective post-surgical diagnostic results rather than relying solely on pre-operative expectations [Data: Entities (571, 2759